config MK2_BLE_DIAG
    bool "MK2: BLE HID diagnostics (notify-failure counters + conn interval capture)"
    default n
    help
      Phase 0 instrumentation for the BLE-only mouse-lag / cursor-jump /
      stuck-key investigation. Counts bt_gatt_notify_cb() outcomes per HID pipe
      and caches the active peripheral connection interval so USB-unplugged
      sessions can be analyzed from the trackball data-logger dump. Adds no GATT
      characteristics and changes no connection behavior (no settings_reset
      required). Leave off for release builds.

config MK2_HOLD_TAP_CAPTURE_STATS
    bool "MK2: count hold-tap captured-event overflows"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_BEHAVIOR_HOLD_TAP
    help
      While a hold-tap is undecided, every other key event is parked in
      captured_events[] and replayed once the hold/tap question is settled.
      If that array is full the event is dropped, yet the listener still
      answers ZMK_EV_EVENT_CAPTURED — telling the event system it was taken
      when it was not. A dropped key release never comes back, which leaves
      that key held forever.

      Whether this actually happens is unknown: nothing counts it today.
      This adds two counters — overflows, and the high water mark of the
      array — so the question can be answered from a real keyboard instead
      of by reading the code. Measurement only: the drop still happens
      exactly as before, so behaviour is unchanged.

      Printed every MK2_HOLD_TAP_CAPTURE_STATS_DUMP_MS as [HTGUARD] on the
      console, and only while a host asserts DTR. Leave off for release.

config MK2_HOLD_TAP_CAPTURE_STATS_DUMP_MS
    int "MK2: [HTGUARD] readout interval (ms)"
    default 10000
    depends on MK2_HOLD_TAP_CAPTURE_STATS
    help
      Matches the [NGUARD] cadence so both readouts line up in one log.

config MK2_HOG_MOUSE_ACC
    bool "MK2: coalesce BLE mouse reports into a delta accumulator (fixes cursor lag/jumps)"
    depends on ZMK_POINTING
    default n
    help
      Phase 4 fix for BLE cursor lag + jumps. Replaces the fixed-depth mouse HID
      msgq with a small button-segmented delta accumulator: the 250 Hz trackball
      backlog is coalesced into one fresh relative-delta report per send
      opportunity, self-paced by the BLE TX-buffer wait. Eliminates the queue
      backpressure that stalls the sensor poll and the stale-delta replay that
      shows as cursor warp under congestion. R/Central only (mouse pipe). No GATT
      change, no settings_reset. Off restores the original msgq behavior.

config MK2_SPLIT_LINK_STATS
    bool "MK2: split link connect/disconnect statistics"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT && ZMK_SPLIT_BLE && ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL && MK2_BLE_DIAG
    help
      Records why the link to the peripheral went down and how long it stayed down.
      The reported symptom is the left half dropping and returning about three
      seconds later, on some host machines and not others. Three seconds points at
      the link being torn down and re-established rather than the peripheral hanging
      or rebooting, but nothing today records the reason.

      A printk alone was tried in July and captured nothing, because it only helps if
      a console happens to be attached at that instant. These counters hold the last
      reason, the counts and the shortest observed outage until somebody asks for them
      through the [MK2_DIAG] footer - so the cable can be attached afterwards, and so
      the same readout works on a customer's keyboard.

      reason is the HCI error code: 0x08 supervision timeout (the halves stopped
      hearing each other), 0x13/0x16 one side closed the link deliberately, 0x3e a
      connection attempt never completed. Radio problems and protocol problems call
      for opposite fixes, and this is what tells them apart.

      Measurement only: no GATT change, no settings_reset, no change to connection
      handling. Depends on MK2_BLE_DIAG because that footer is the only readout path.

config MK2_HOST_LINK_STATS
    bool "MK2: host link connect/disconnect statistics"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_BLE && MK2_BLE_DIAG
    help
      Records the link to the host going down, coming back, or failing to come up at
      all. MK2_SPLIT_LINK_STATS covers the link between the halves; this covers the
      other hop, which nothing has measured until now.

      The gap this closes: every existing counter reports whether a notification
      succeeded, and a notification can only fail if something was sent. A link that
      drops while nobody is typing produces no send, so it leaves every counter at
      zero - which is what happened on 2026-08-15 when the owner watched the keyboard
      disappear from the host and the readout afterwards showed nothing at all. A zero
      there means "not measured", not "did not happen", and that is precisely the
      confusion these counters exist to remove.

      fail= is the branch with no counter anywhere: connected() invoked with a non-zero
      error, i.e. the host tried to attach and could not. That is the literal customer
      report ("cannot connect over BT"), and today it returns early leaving no trace.

      reason is the HCI error code: 0x08 supervision timeout (the two ends stopped
      hearing each other - a radio problem), 0x13/0x16 one end closed the link
      deliberately (a host or protocol decision, and what a normal profile switch looks
      like), 0x3e a connection attempt that never completed. Radio and protocol faults
      call for opposite fixes, so the count alone would not be actionable.

      linked= is read from the connection parameters rather than from these counters, so
      a miswired callback shows up as a disagreement between the two instead of as a
      silent zero. It reflects the active profile only.

      Measurement only: no GATT change, no settings_reset, no change to connection or
      advertising handling. Depends on MK2_BLE_DIAG because that footer is the only
      readout path.

config MK2_SPLIT_POS_DROP_STATS
    bool "MK2: split position drop statistics"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT && ZMK_SPLIT_BLE && ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL && MK2_BLE_DIAG
    help
      Measurement-only counters for dropped split position events on the Central.
      This does not change keyboard behavior, ordering, or retry handling.
      Depends on MK2_BLE_DIAG because the only readout path is the [MK2_DIAG]
      footer: without it the counters would tick but never be printed, which
      would silently look like "no drops".

config MK2_SPLIT_POS_DROP_SELFTEST
    int "MK2: split position drop self-test interval"
    default 0
    range 0 10000
    depends on MK2_SPLIT_POS_DROP_STATS
    help
      Bench calibration only. Drops every Nth peripheral key position event
      before it is queued, which both bumps the counters and reproduces a stuck
      key, proving the instrument works before any field measurement is trusted.
      FAULT INJECTION - zero disables it and leaves behavior unchanged.

config MK2_SPLIT_NOTIFY_STATS
    bool "MK2: split notification statistics"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT && ZMK_SPLIT_BLE && !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    select ZMK_LOW_PRIORITY_WORK_QUEUE
    help
      Measurement-only counters for split position notifications on the Peripheral.
      This does not change keyboard behavior, ordering, or retry handling.
      The readout needs a console the host can assert DTR on; on a peripheral
      CONFIG_ZMK_USB is unavailable, so the board overlay must point
      chosen zephyr,console at a cdc-acm node itself.

config MK2_SPLIT_NOTIFY_STATS_DUMP_MS
    int "MK2: split notification statistics dump interval"
    default 10000
    range 100 3600000
    depends on MK2_SPLIT_NOTIFY_STATS
    help
      Measurement-only periodic dump interval in milliseconds. The dump remains
      silent unless a ready console has an attached USB host with DTR asserted.

config MK2_SPLIT_NOTIFY_STATS_SELFTEST
    int "MK2: split notification statistics self-test interval"
    default 0
    range 0 10000
    depends on MK2_SPLIT_NOTIFY_STATS
    help
      Measurement-only bench calibration that treats every Nth notification as
      -ENOMEM without sending it. Zero disables it and leaves behavior unchanged.
      FAULT INJECTION - a skipped notification can leave a key or layer stuck
      exactly like the defect under investigation, so never ship this enabled.

config MK2_KSCAN_DROP_STATS
    bool "MK2: local kscan edge loss, queue wait, and scan liveness"
    default n
    depends on ZMK_KSCAN
    help
      The local key scan callback puts each press/release edge on a fixed-depth
      queue with K_NO_WAIT and never looks at the result. A full queue therefore
      discards the edge in silence, and the scan driver only reports transitions,
      so a discarded release is never re-sent: that key stays held forever.

      This path exists on BOTH halves and had no instrument of any kind. Every
      counter built so far for the stuck-key hunt lives on the central and looks
      only at events that arrived over the split link, so a loss here is invisible
      to all of them - which is how a symptomatic session can report zero drops.

      Two things are added. Recovery: when the queue is full the OLDEST queued
      edge is evicted so the newest survives, because losing a release (key held
      forever) is far worse than losing a press (one missing character). Same
      idiom as the HID send queue in hog.c. Measurement: eviction and outright
      loss are counted separately, the last eight events are kept with position,
      press/release, error and how long the consumer had been idle, and the set
      of positions this half believes are held is printed alongside. If that set
      shows a key down that the fingers have released, the fault is at or before
      key scanning, not on the split link.

      Read out as [KGUARD] - through the [MK2_DIAG] footer on the central, and
      through the periodic [NGUARD] dump on the peripheral.

config MK2_BLE_DIAG_AUTO_DUMP_MS
    int "MK2: automatic [MK2_DIAG] footer interval in ms (0 = manual key only)"
    default 0
    range 0 3600000
    depends on MK2_BLE_DIAG
    help
      Prints the whole diagnostic footer on a timer instead of only when the DUMP
      key is pressed. The manual key cannot capture the symptom under study: the
      DUMP binding lives on the left half, and the fault being chased is the left
      half becoming unable to send key events. Three captures were lost that way.

      Zero keeps the previous manual-only behavior. Any non-zero value still
      prints nothing unless a host has the console open with DTR asserted, so a
      keyboard in normal use is unaffected.

config ZMK_BLE_CLEAR_BONDS_ON_START
    bool "Configuration that clears all bond information from the keyboard on startup."

# HID GATT notifications sent this way are *not* picked up by Linux, and possibly others.
config BT_GATT_NOTIFY_MULTIPLE
    default n

config BT_GATT_AUTO_SEC_REQ
    default (ZMK_SPLIT_BLE && !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL)

config BT_DEVICE_APPEARANCE
    default 961

config BT_PERIPHERAL_PREF_MIN_INT
    default 6

config BT_PERIPHERAL_PREF_MAX_INT
    default 12

config BT_PERIPHERAL_PREF_LATENCY
    default 30

config BT_PERIPHERAL_PREF_TIMEOUT
    default 400

# The device name should be 16 characters or less so it fits within the
# advertising data.
